The memo key didn't work — 2 fetches. Why? `ioCacheGet(owner, repo, 'prVerdicts', memoId)` — the ioCacheKey function: `owner + '/' + repo + ':' + kind + (id ? ':' + id : '')`. Hmm, the memoized lookup should hit on second call... unless `Date.now()` — no, same tick.

Wait — the loadWith helper: each `loadWith` call creates a NEW provider via `mod.createSmProvider(...)`. That's fine, module-level _cache shared. In my test I call `p.verdictRecords(5, {authorLogins})` then `p.verdictRecords(5)` — both on the same provider p. First call: memoId = "5|aw:ai-teammate" → miss → fetch (calls=1) → put. Second: memoId = "5" → miss → fetch (calls=2).

Oh wait — my test asserts `calls.length == 1` after BOTH calls. But the two variants have different memo keys → two fetches! My test expectation is wrong: the unfiltered and filtered variants genuinely need separate parses (different outputs), so they can't share one fetch... unless we cache the RAW comment list and parse per allowlist. 

Better design: cache the raw comment list fetch, parse per call. Restructure: use a separate cache kind 'prComments' for the raw payload, then parse on every verdictRecords call (parsing is cheap; the expensive part is the API fetch). That preserves the memo test contract "three reads, one fetch" and makes my new test's "one comment fetch" assertion correct.

Let me restructure verdictRecords:

```js
verdictRecords: function (prNumber, opts) {
    var allow = (opts && Array.isArray(opts.authorLogins)) ? opts.authorLogins : null;
